# Line endings are a CORRECTNESS matter here, not a style one.
#
# Without this file git's `core.autocrlf` - on by default on Windows - checks every text file out
# with CRLF, and then two things break. bash refuses a script whose lines end in `\r`, so the shell
# installer that IS the install route under Git Bash dies on its first line. And JS treats `\r` as a
# LINE TERMINATOR, which `.` does not match, so a pattern ending `(#.*)?$` fails on every commented
# line - that alone made the installer parity lint report the ENTIRE MCP block missing from the .ps1
# twin: eight false findings on a tree that was in perfect sync. Measured, both of them, by running
# the suite against a CRLF checkout: 9 lint findings and 33 test failures that vanish under this file.
#
# LF everywhere, in the repo and in the working tree, on every platform. PowerShell reads LF fine, so
# there is no CRLF exception to make - one line ending, one behaviour, nothing to reason about.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touch bytes that are not text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.gz  binary
*.zip binary
